I read an article that also walks through React's evolution. I have always believed every technical approach exists to solve problems better.

## How React components evolved

React components roughly fall into three stages:

- createClass Components
- Class Components
- Function Components

### createClass Components

Example:

```javascript
const Index = React.createClass({
  getDefaultProps() {
    return {};
  },
  getInitialState() {
    return {
      name: "robin",
    };
  },
  render() {
    return <div></div>;
  },
});
```

This was the early way to create components — I assume JavaScript did not yet have a built-in class system. When ES6 arrived, everything changed.

### Class Components

Example:

```javascript
class Index extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      name: "robin",
    };
  }

  render() {
    return <div></div>;
  }
}
```

How you initialize props and state changed with it. You might ask: if `createClass` worked fine, why switch to class inheritance? My guess is it was about riding the ES language wave.

As projects grew more complex, building everything as classes started to feel heavy. Then this arrived.

### Function Components

Example:

```javascript
const Button = (props) => {
  return <div></div>;
};
```

If I only need a shared presentational piece, this is enough.

Dark and stormy night… while building components…

![image]()

Compound components like that need state — but reaching for a full class component feels like overkill. So React Hooks were born.

## Introducing React Hooks

- useState()
- useContext()
- useReducer()
- useEffect()

### useState

When you need to work with state:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";

export default function TextInput() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return <input value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

### useContext

When you need shared state:

```javascript
const AppContext = React.createContext({});

const Button1 = () => {
  const { name } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <div>{name}</div>;
};
const Button2 = () => {
  const { name, age } = useContext(AppContext);

  return (
    <div>
      {name}
      {age}
    </div>
  );
};

const Button3 = () => {
  const [name, setName] = useState("robin111");
  return (
    <AppContext.Provider
      value={{
        name: name,
        age: 29,
      }}
    >
      <Button1 />
      <Button2 />
      <button onClick={() => setName("test")}>211</button>
    </AppContext.Provider>
  );
};
```

When components nest under shared context, the current value comes from the nearest `<AppContext.Provider>` above, via its `value` prop.

### useReducer

Redux offers a state-management model; you can use the same idea with `useReducer`.

```javascript
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialArg, init);
```

A counter example inspired by Ruan Yifeng:

```javascript
const myReducer = (state, action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "countUp":
      return {
        ...state,
        count: state.count + 1,
      };
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

function App() {
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(myReducer, { count: 0 });
  return (
    <div className="App">
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: "countUp" })}>+1</button>
      <p>Count: {state.count}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### useEffect

Official description: the Effect Hook lets you run side effects in function components.

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

This is the official example — short and clear.

The docs cover Effects in depth; strongly recommended reading. See the Chinese community deep dives as well.

### Custom Hooks

The point of custom Hooks is to package repeated logic and share it. A custom Hook is not just a plain function wrap — the logic inside still needs official Hooks for it to count. See the Chinese community write-ups for more.

## Summary

I have only started using Hooks in projects and have not gone deep yet — so I can only list the patterns I can actually reach for. Custom Hooks, as I see them, are composition and packaging of Hooks.

